-- Dropping these tables disconnects every connected app: all OAuth clients,
-- grants and tokens are discarded and people must connect again. Cleanups
-- waiting for a confirmation are discarded, and Activity stops naming the
-- app an earlier action came from. No Gmail data, action history or undo
-- state lives here.
--
-- product_events keeps the seven connector event names and the wider
-- props CHECK: Postgres cannot drop an enum label, both only widen what
-- the table accepts, and code without the connector writes none of them.
-- Connector rows already recorded stay until the 13-month retention.
--
-- Roll back 0102 (activity_origins) before this one: its foreign keys
-- point at the OAuth tables dropped here.
--
-- ROLL THE CODE BACK FIRST. Account deletion and the daily retention worker
-- run statements against these tables whether or not MCP_ENABLED is on, so
-- dropping them under the code that shipped with this migration breaks both.
--
-- One transaction, so a failure part-way leaves the schema whole. Dropping
-- these tables removes foreign-key triggers from users, workspaces,
-- mailbox_accounts and action_jobs, which needs a brief exclusive lock on
-- each: bound the wait so a long reader fails this rollback instead of
-- queueing every query behind it.
--
-- IF EXISTS: a dev database that applied an earlier draft of 0101 lacks
-- some of these tables, and this is how it is repaired.
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '3s';
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS action_origins;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS action_previews;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS oauth_interactions;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS oauth_auth_codes;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS oauth_tokens;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS oauth_grants;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS oauth_clients;
COMMIT;
